+# Decision: Preview Cache Replacement Policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker maintains a bounded 12 GiB private cache of rendered JPEG contact sheets. Two distinct usage patterns drive load: long sequential screenings, in which an editor steps through hundreds of clips seen once and likely never again, and focused comparison sessions, in which a small working set of sheets is revisited repeatedly. The replacement policy must protect comparison sheets during screenings while respecting the single-machine budget. No other node pins entries, inspects policy counters, or sees eviction events; the policy is entirely internal to the worker.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gate — no change to the external interface.** The replacement policy is hidden behind `GET /internal/previews/{clip-id}/{revision}`. Any option that leaves the 200/202/404 contract and the FIFO render queue unchanged passes. All three candidates pass.
+
+**Criterion 1 — screening-scan resistance (major).** A long screening populates the cache with hundreds of one-use sheets. The policy must not allow that scan to evict the comparison sheets an editor returns to across sessions. This is the criterion that most directly determines whether the cache serves its purpose on the observed workload.
+
+**Criterion 2 — bookkeeping cost (major).** The worker runs on one eight-core machine shared with the render queue. Per-request metadata overhead competes with rendering throughput at 450 ms per sheet.
+
+**Criterion 3 — adaptation after a project switch (moderate).** Editors routinely switch between projects. Entries from a finished project should lose priority within a reasonable number of subsequent cache fills, not linger indefinitely.
+
+**Criterion 4 — tuning surface (minor).** Parameters that must be calibrated before production add operational burden. Fewer startup parameters, or parameters with interpretable defaults, reduce this cost.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+**Description.** A single doubly-linked list ordered by recency of access, with a hash map from cache key to node. On every hit, move the node to the MRU end. On every admission, evict from the LRU end until there is room. No state beyond the list and the map; no startup parameters.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Gate — external interface unchanged: **passes**.
+- Criterion 1 — screening-scan resistance: **weak**. A sequential screening of N sheets evicts the N least-recently-used entries, which are the comparison sheets that haven't been touched during the screening. The longer the screening, the more comparison work is displaced.
+- Criterion 2 — bookkeeping cost: **strong**. O(1) per hit and per admission; one pointer-pair update per access.
+- Criterion 3 — adaptation after project switch: **strong**. Stale entries fall to the LRU end naturally as new work arrives; a project switch clears old favorites within one cache-volume of new accesses.
+- Criterion 4 — tuning surface: **strong**. No parameters beyond the total size limit.
+
+**Summary.** Minimum bookkeeping, fast adaptation — but offers no protection for reused entries against scan traffic.
+
+---
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description.** Each cached entry carries an access counter. On a hit, increment the counter. Periodically (or on eviction), decay all counters by a factor (e.g., halve them). On eviction, remove the entry with the lowest decayed frequency. Counter decay prevents entries from accumulating unbounded counts that would make them immune to eviction after a project switch.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Gate — external interface unchanged: **passes**.
+- Criterion 1 — screening-scan resistance: **partial**. An entry seen many times before a screening retains a high counter and resists eviction. However, if a screening run is long enough, newly admitted screening entries accumulate counts that can rival the comparison entries' aged counts, and the protection erodes. The degree of protection depends on the decay rate and the ratio of screening length to comparison-session frequency.
+- Criterion 2 — bookkeeping cost: **partial**. Each hit requires a counter increment (cheap) and each eviction a linear scan or a priority queue update. Periodic aging requires traversing all cached entries — O(n) — or maintaining a sorted structure — O(log n) per update.
+- Criterion 3 — adaptation after project switch: **partial**. Decay reduces stale counts over time, but the lag is proportional to how large the counts grew. A heavily used entry from yesterday's project retains elevated frequency for longer than a newer entry from today's.
+- Criterion 4 — tuning surface: **weak**. The decay rate is a free parameter; too aggressive and the policy degrades toward LRU, too conservative and stale favorites persist. Calibrating it for a workload that mixes short comparisons with long screenings is non-obvious.
+
+**Summary.** Better retention of popular clips than LRU, but counter maintenance and the decay-rate parameter add work and can leave yesterday's favorites entrenched.
+
+---
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description.** Two queues partition the cache: A1, a FIFO probationary queue with a fixed size limit (default 3 GiB), and Am, an LRU main queue with the remaining capacity (default 9 GiB). A newly admitted entry enters A1. When a request hits an A1 entry, it is promoted to the MRU end of Am. A request hitting an Am entry moves it to the MRU end of Am. Eviction targets A1's head (oldest probationary entry) when A1 is at its size limit; it targets Am's LRU end when Am would exceed its capacity after a promotion. One startup parameter: the A1 size limit.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Gate — external interface unchanged: **passes**. Changes are confined to the replacement-tracking data structures inside the preview worker.
+- Criterion 1 — screening-scan resistance: **strong**. Screening sheets enter A1 and cycle through it FIFO. Unless an editor revisits a screening sheet (promoting it to Am), screening traffic never reaches Am. Comparison sheets, revisited across sessions, live in Am and are not directly displaced by screening load — Am can only be affected if A1 fills and the total cache reaches its ceiling, causing Am-LRU eviction to make room for a promotion.
+- Criterion 2 — bookkeeping cost: **partial**. Two queue data structures instead of one; O(1) per hit and per admission (hash map to find the entry, pointer updates to move it). No decay traversal. The overhead relative to LRU is one extra queue and one extra pointer check per operation — constant-factor, not asymptotically worse.
+- Criterion 3 — adaptation after project switch: **strong** for Am, **strong** for A1. Am is LRU, so entries from a finished project fall toward the LRU end as new comparison work arrives. A1 is FIFO, so old probationary entries age out directly.
+- Criterion 4 — tuning surface: **partial**. One parameter (A1 size limit) with an interpretable meaning: "how many one-use sheets can a screening add before scan pressure reaches Am." The 3 GiB default accommodates roughly 1,500 screening sheets at the typical 2 MiB per sheet — a concrete, legible starting point.
+
+**Summary.** Strong scan resistance with moderate bookkeeping cost and one interpretable tuning parameter.
+
+## Selection
+
+**2Q** is chosen.
+
+The decision turns on Criterion 1 (screening-scan resistance, major). LRU fails it directly: every screening sheet evicted is a comparison sheet displaced, and the workload — long screenings interleaved with focused comparisons — is exactly the pattern LRU handles worst. Frequency-with-aging offers partial resistance, but that protection depends on the decay rate and erodes during long screenings; it also introduces the hardest parameter to calibrate (decay rate) in exchange for the partial benefit.
+
+2Q addresses the criterion structurally rather than statistically: screening sheets are physically separated into A1 and cannot reach Am without a second access. The protection is unconditional on workload shape.
+
+The cost is Criterion 2 (bookkeeping cost, major): 2Q is not as cheap as LRU. The actual overhead is one extra queue and a constant-factor increase per operation; it does not introduce O(n) traversal or a sorted structure. On a machine where rendering itself costs 450 ms, the per-request pointer update difference is not the binding constraint.
+
+On Criterion 3 (adaptation, moderate), 2Q is as good as LRU: Am is LRU, so project-switch adaptation in the main queue is identical. On Criterion 4 (tuning surface, minor), 2Q's one parameter (A1 size limit) is more interpretable than the frequency decay rate it avoids.
+
+This decision commits to the two-queue data structure and the A1/Am split. It forecloses the simpler LRU implementation. It obligates the LLD to specify the A1 capacity parameter, its default, and its valid range, and to describe eviction behavior during both admission and promotion. It does not obligate any change to the external HTTP interface, the render queue, or the disk layout.
+
+The tenet engaged: *Keep cache paths and replacement bookkeeping private to the preview worker.* 2Q satisfies it fully — all policy state is internal and invisible to the review API.
