 ## Architecture
 
@@ -23,4 +23,36 @@ Resolve the immutable source revision before rendering. The cache key includes c
 
 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The cache uses **2Q replacement**. Two internal queues partition the occupied space:
+
+- **A1 (probationary queue)**: a FIFO queue holding entries seen exactly once. Its capacity is configurable at startup; the default is 3 GiB (25% of the 12 GiB ceiling). At the typical 2 MiB per sheet this accommodates roughly 1,500 screening sheets before A1 itself must evict.
+- **Am (main queue)**: an LRU queue holding entries that have been accessed more than once. Am's capacity is the total ceiling minus the A1 size limit (9 GiB at defaults). Because the two limits sum to the total ceiling, the total cannot be exceeded without one queue being at its own limit.
+
+**Admission.** A completed sheet not already cached is admitted to the tail of A1. If A1 is at its configured size limit, one or more A1 entries are evicted from the head (oldest) until there is room.
+
+**Promotion.** A cache hit on an A1 entry moves that entry to the MRU end of Am. Because the entry was already counted in the total, total occupancy does not change. However, if Am's occupancy after receiving the entry would exceed Am's own capacity, one or more LRU entries are evicted from Am before the promotion completes. (Am's capacity equals the total ceiling minus the A1 size limit — 9 GiB at the defaults.)
+
+**Cache hit in Am.** Move the entry to the MRU end of Am; no change in total size.
+
+**Screening-scan resistance.** Sequential screening fills A1 without promoting to Am. Because A1 is FIFO-evicted before Am is touched, screening runs cannot directly evict comparison sheets from Am. Sheets revisited across sessions promote into Am and remain there until Am itself fills.
+
+The A1 capacity parameter is read at startup. Increasing it tolerates longer screening runs before any A1 eviction forces cascading Am pressure (by pushing total occupancy to the ceiling); decreasing it reserves more headroom for comparison work. The default of 3 GiB is a reasonable starting point for the pilot load; it is expected to be tuned once production workload data is available.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU is simple but is vulnerable to sequential screening scans evicting comparison sheets directly from the single queue. Aged frequency counters protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter state and can retain stale favorites after a project switch. 2Q isolates first-use entries in A1 so screening scans cannot reach Am. The bookkeeping cost is two queues plus a startup size parameter. See `decisions/eviction-policy.md`. |
+| A1 default capacity | 3 GiB (25% of 12 GiB) | 10–50% variants | Fits ~1,500 typical screening sheets before A1 self-evicts; leaves 9 GiB for comparison work. Startup-configurable; expected to be tuned from pilot data. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Eviction policy: 2Q with configurable A1 capacity.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether the A1 capacity parameter should be expressed as a ratio of the total limit rather than an absolute byte count, so that changing the 12 GiB ceiling does not silently unbalance the split.
+2. Whether per-queue occupancy and hit rates are exposed through worker health metrics.
+3. Whether the A1 capacity parameter is reloadable at runtime without a restart.
